.TH SPORTS-GAME-LAUNCHERS 7 "2026-09-13" "sports_near_me 0.1.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
sports-game-launchers \- per-platform scripts that run sports-game(1)
without a manual install
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scripts/sports-game.sh
.RI [ args ...]
.RI ( macOS/Linux )
.PP
.B scripts\esports-game.cmd
.RI [ args ...]
.RI ( "Windows cmd.exe" )
.PP
.B scripts/sports-game.ps1
.RI [ args ...]
.RI ( "PowerShell, Windows or cross-platform" )
.SH DESCRIPTION
These three scripts are equivalent entry points into
.BR sports-game (1)
for someone who does not want to run
.B pip install
by hand. Each one, on first run only:
.IP 1. 4
Creates a virtual environment at
.I .venv
next to the project root, if one is not already there.
.IP 2. 4
Installs this package into it (editable install), along with its one
dependency, PyYAML.
.PP
On every run \(em first or subsequent \(em the script then execs
.BR sports-game (1)
inside that virtual environment, forwarding every argument unchanged.
There is no functional difference between running a launcher script and
running
.B sports-game
directly after a manual
.BR "pip install \-e ." ;
the scripts exist purely so a non-technical user can double-click or
run one command on whichever platform they have, without knowing what a
virtual environment is.
.SH USAGE
The argument forwarding means every
.BR sports-game (1)
invocation shown in that man page works identically through any
launcher, e.g.:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./scripts/sports\-game.sh nfl bears
scripts\esports\-game.cmd nfl bears
\&./scripts/sports\-game.ps1 nfl bears
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .venv/
The bootstrapped virtual environment. Safe to delete at any time; the
next launcher run recreates it.
.SH PLATFORM NOTES
.TP
.B sports-game.sh
Requires
.B python3
(or
.BR python )
on
.BR PATH .
Uses
.B set \-euo pipefail
so a setup failure stops the script rather than falling through to a
broken run.
.TP
.B sports-game.cmd
Requires
.B python
on
.BR PATH .
Each setup step is checked; a failure prints a one-line hint about
Python/PATH rather than a raw traceback.
.TP
.B sports-game.ps1
Works under both Windows PowerShell 5.1 and cross-platform PowerShell
7+ (including on macOS/Linux, where it is a third option alongside the
.B .sh
script). Detects the platform via
.I $env:OS
rather than the newer
.I $IsWindows
automatic variable, since the latter does not exist under Windows
PowerShell 5.1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sports-game (1),
.BR sports_near_me.yaml (5)
